A thorough investigation of every component cited in the ticket finds no fault. The DNS server (dns_server, 10.200.0.2) is running BIND 9 (named, active/running), is authoritative for the "local" zone, and correctly answers queries for all four web-server hostnames: web0.local → 10.200.0.3, web1.local → 10.200.0.4, web2.local → 10.200.0.5, web3.local → 10.200.0.6. Name resolution from host_1_1_1_1 succeeds end-to-end via the system resolver (getent returns correct addresses for all four names), and HTTP requests to each web server over its resolved IP return HTTP 200. The host's /etc/resolv.conf correctly points to 10.200.0.2, and /etc/nsswitch.conf uses "files dns" with no mDNS interception of .local names. OSPF is fully converged across the core: router_core_1 carries a valid route to 10.200.0.0/24 via router_core_3 and the server-access switch, with 0% packet loss on every tested path. The claim that "the name server must be dead" is incorrect — it was a premature conclusion not supported by any evidence in the live lab. The network is operating normally; no corrective action is required. If the end user experienced intermittent failures at a specific earlier time, the most likely transient cause would have been OSPF convergence delay immediately after a lab restart (routes settle in under three minutes), but the topology is fully stable now.
